{%- comment -%}
  On Google — the map, the live rating, and (only if an API key exists) the
  review text and photos.

  TWO TIERS, deliberately. The Place ID alone is enough for the map card, and
  Google renders the star rating and review count inside it — live, no key, no
  billing, nothing to maintain. That is the section's floor and it is a real
  section on its own. An API key adds the review TEXT and the listing photos on
  this page; without one the visitor gets the map and a link, which is honest
  and costs nothing.

  Google returns AT MOST FIVE reviews even with a key, and does not let you
  choose which — the "all reviews" link is not a nicety, it is the only route
  to the rest.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign has_id = false
  if settings.google_place_id != blank
    assign has_id = true
  endif
  assign has_api = false
  if has_id and settings.google_maps_key != blank
    assign has_api = true
  endif
  # /maps/search/?api=1 is the DOCUMENTED Maps URLs form and the one that opens
  # the Maps app on a phone. /maps/place/?q=place_id: is undocumented and errors
  # on mobile — reported from a real device. Do not change it back.
  if settings.google_maps_url != blank
    assign maps_url = settings.google_maps_url
  elsif has_id
    assign gq = settings.google_map_query | default: shop.name | url_encode
    assign maps_url = 'https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=' | append: gq | append: '&query_place_id=' | append: settings.google_place_id
  else
    assign maps_url = '#'
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  With no API key and the map switched off there is nothing left to show but a
  heading, so the section stands down rather than leaving a 240px gap — which is
  exactly what it did on the contact page, where the contact panel already
  carries the map.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign has_something = false
  if has_api or section.settings.show_map
    assign has_something = true
  endif
-%}
<section class="section grevs {{ section.settings.bg }}" data-gp-section
         {% unless has_id and has_something or request.design_mode %}hidden{% endunless %}>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        {%- if section.settings.eyebrow != blank -%}
          <p class="eyebrow">{{ section.settings.eyebrow }}</p>
        {%- endif -%}
        <h2 class="h1" data-split>{{ section.settings.heading }}</h2>

        {%- comment -%}
          Suppressed whenever the map card is on, because that card carries a
          better version of the same thing a few lines below — with the khatam,
          the count and its own link. Two identical score rows stacked is what
          shipped for one deploy and it read as a mistake.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- liquid
          assign show_head_score = false
          if has_api or settings.google_rating != blank
            assign show_head_score = true
          endif
          if section.settings.show_map
            assign show_head_score = false
          endif
        -%}
        <div class="grevs__score"{% unless show_head_score %} hidden{% endunless %}>
          <span data-gp="stars">
            {%- if settings.google_rating != blank -%}
              <span class="gstars" role="img" aria-label="{{ settings.google_rating }} out of 5">
                <span class="gstars__bg">★★★★★</span>
                <span class="gstars__fg" style="width:{{ settings.google_rating | times: 20.0 }}%">★★★★★</span>
              </span>
            {%- endif -%}
          </span>
          <span class="grevs__num">
            <strong data-gp="rating">{{ settings.google_rating }}</strong>
            <span class="grevs__of">/ 5</span>
          </span>
          <span class="grevs__count">
            <span data-gp="count">{{ settings.google_review_count }}</span> Google reviews
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>

      {%- comment -%}
        Same reasoning: the map card's own actions row already offers "Reviews on
        Google", so this would be the third route to one page in one section.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- unless section.settings.show_map -%}
        <a class="link-arrow" data-gp="maps" href="{{ maps_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          {{ section.settings.link_label }} {% render 'icon', name: 'arrow' %}
        </a>
      {%- endunless -%}
    </div>

    {%- if section.settings.show_map and has_id -%}
      {% render 'google-map', height: section.settings.map_height %}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      Hidden until the fetch lands, then unhidden by the script. The rail
      primitive does the mobile work — reviews are browsable peer items, which
      is exactly what a rail is for. Stays hidden forever without an API key,
      which is the intended state, not a failure.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="grid grid--reviews rail" data-gp-reviews
         data-min-rating="{{ section.settings.min_rating }}" hidden></div>

    {%- if section.settings.show_photos -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        PHOTOS. These are snapshots taken in a shop, not product photography —
        different phones, different light, different years. Presented as a
        contact sheet rather than as hero imagery: one uniform square crop, one
        scrolling strip, a shared warm grade so mismatched white balance stops
        fighting, and a caption that says plainly where they came from. The
        merchant can skip the first N if the listing leads with a weak one, and
        turn the whole strip off in one click.

        Google requires the photographer's name to be shown; it is rendered
        under each frame.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="gshots" data-gp-needs-data hidden>
        <p class="gshots__label">
          {{ section.settings.photos_label }}
        </p>
        <div class="gshots__strip grid rail" data-gp-photos
             data-max="{{ section.settings.photos_count }}"
             data-skip="{{ section.settings.photos_skip }}" hidden></div>
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    <p class="grevs__source">
      {%- if has_api -%}
        Ratings and reviews come straight from our Google Business Profile and
        refresh on their own. Google shows at most five here —
        <a data-gp="maps" href="{{ maps_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">read them all on Google</a>.
      {%- elsif section.settings.show_map -%}
        Every rating and review is on our Google Business Profile, and the map is
        drawn by Google itself.
      {%- else -%}
        <a data-gp="maps" href="{{ maps_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the reviews on Google</a>.
      {%- endif -%}
    </p>

    {%- if request.design_mode -%}
      {%- if has_id == false -%}
        <p class="grevs__setup">
          <strong>No Place ID yet.</strong> Add it under
          <strong>Theme settings → Google Business Profile</strong>. Until then
          this section is hidden from visitors and only visible here in the editor.
        </p>
      {%- elsif has_api == false -%}
        <p class="grevs__setup">
          <strong>Map and live rating are on.</strong> Review text and photos on
          this page need a Places API key, which needs a Google Cloud project
          with billing. Without one, everything above is exactly what visitors
          see — and Google keeps the rating on the map current by itself.
        </p>
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endif -%}

  </div>
</section>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Google reviews",
  "settings": [
    { "type": "text", "id": "eyebrow", "label": "Eyebrow", "default": "From the shop floor" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "heading", "label": "Heading", "default": "What people say" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "link_label", "label": "Link label", "default": "All reviews on Google" },
    { "type": "range", "id": "min_rating", "label": "Hide reviews below", "min": 0, "max": 5, "step": 1, "default": 0,
      "info": "0 shows every review Google returns. Hiding poor reviews is legal but visitors notice a wall of five stars — 0 or 3 is the honest setting." },
    { "type": "header", "content": "Map" },
    { "type": "checkbox", "id": "show_map", "label": "Show the map", "default": true,
      "info": "Google draws the card, including the current star rating and review count. No API key involved." },
    { "type": "range", "id": "map_height", "label": "Map height", "min": 240, "max": 520, "step": 20, "default": 340 },
    { "type": "header", "content": "Photos from Google" },
    { "type": "checkbox", "id": "show_photos", "label": "Show photos", "default": true,
      "info": "Needs the optional API key. Nothing renders without one." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "photos_label", "label": "Caption", "default": "Photographed in the shop by customers and by us" },
    { "type": "range", "id": "photos_count", "label": "How many", "min": 3, "max": 12, "step": 1, "default": 8 },
    { "type": "range", "id": "photos_skip", "label": "Skip the first", "min": 0, "max": 10, "step": 1, "default": 0,
      "info": "Google decides the order and it is not always the best one first. Skip past a weak opener." },
    { "type": "header", "content": "Layout" },
    { "type": "select", "id": "bg", "label": "Background", "default": "section--shell",
      "options": [
        { "value": "", "label": "Paper" },
        { "value": "section--shell", "label": "Shell" }
      ] }
  ],
  "presets": [{ "name": "Google reviews" }]
}
{% endschema %}
